WEBVTT

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:54.190
understand zeros and ones, right?

00:14:54.190 --> 00:14:57.100
They only understand the
so-called binary system.

00:14:57.100 --> 00:15:00.220
And yet, this does not quite
look like zeros and ones.

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:03.910
So how is it that I, who can just
barely understand this as a human,

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:07.060
can program a computer using
this and have it understand this,

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:09.560
even though these, themselves,
are not zeros and ones?

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:14.110
In other words, how do we
get this to that instead?

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:16.300
Well, it turns out when
writing a computer program,

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:17.874
there's often multiple steps.

00:15:17.874 --> 00:15:19.540
And sometimes these steps are automated.

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:22.840
But sometimes you have to type, you
have to make them happen manually.

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:26.560
And in general, the process
of converting this code

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:30.580
here, or source code, which tends
to be English-like syntax that

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:35.530
does follow a set of standards and use
a certain vocabulary, this being C,

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:41.710
the language known as C, I can convert
this source code to machine code, that

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:45.310
is zeros and ones that look
like this by running this source

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:49.000
code through a special type of program
quite simply called a Compiler.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:52.300
A compiler is simply a
program that compiles code,

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:56.830
converts it from source code to machine
code so that the human can write it,

00:15:56.830 --> 00:16:00.960
but the machine can
ultimately understand it.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:02.610
Consider another example here.

00:16:02.610 --> 00:16:07.740
This program looks almost kind
of-- well, not really the same.

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:10.819
But it's written in a very
similar language called C++.

00:16:10.819 --> 00:16:13.860
Now, here too, you might not recognize
some of the symbols on the screen.

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:16.800
But you probably do recognize
hello world, this time

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:18.600
without the backslash n.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:22.980
And even though we have a different word
up here, I/O stream and standard, endl

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:26.940
here, odds are it's a pretty
good bet that this program too

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:28.110
does print hello world.

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:31.260
And this is already, in simplest
form, testament to the fact

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:36.330
that many programming languages
do the same things differently.

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:41.670
Indeed, whether you know C or C++ or
something like Java or PHP or Python

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:44.100
or Ruby, or any number of
other languages, odds are,

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:47.700
at the end of the day, you can actually
solve the same problems using any

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:50.850
number of different languages,
much like you, as a human,

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:55.080
can express yourself verbally in
any number of spoken languages.

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:57.630
But it might just be
easier to communicate

00:16:57.630 --> 00:16:59.880
with other humans in one language.

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:02.220
It might just be easier
and less time consuming

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:05.577
to solve problems using one
programming language over another.

00:17:05.577 --> 00:17:08.160
And this indeed, in part at
least, explains why there are just

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:09.450
so many programming languages.

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:10.932
We've seen just two so far.

00:17:10.932 --> 00:17:12.390
But many different languages exist.

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:14.973
Because as more and more time
passes, and more and more humans

00:17:14.973 --> 00:17:18.150
use these languages, do they
realize, wow, I could do this better.

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.770
Or gee, I wish I had this
feature instead or wow,

00:17:20.770 --> 00:17:24.210
I wish I could type fewer keys
on the keyboard in order just

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:26.520
to have my computer say hello world.

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:29.550
And so you'll find that different
languages do things differently

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:31.710
even if the end result is the same.

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:35.580
For instance, here is a
language called Python.

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:39.030
And thank God-- this is the
simplest example of the ones

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:42.000
we've seen thus far-- it
really just says what it means.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:45.510
No hashtags up top, no
curly braces, apparently.

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:47.790
Just print hello world.

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:51.270
And that's with this
program in Python does.

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:53.820
Now Python, it's worth
noting, is a different type

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:57.390
of language in most implementations.

00:17:57.390 --> 00:18:00.750
That is to say, you typically
don't write Python source code

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:04.410
and then manually convert it to
machine code, zeros and ones.

00:18:04.410 --> 00:18:08.490
Rather, there is a special program that
you use that's not called a compiler,

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:10.200
but called an interpreter.

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:13.110
Because what's also the
case, with some languages,

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:15.630
is that rather than convert
them directly to zeros and ones,

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:20.000
that your CPU, your computer's
brain understands directly,

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:23.370
you instead convert them to some
intermediate language, generally known

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:24.360
as bytecode.

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:27.280
Java is the same way, as
are some other languages.

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:29.490
And so with Python,
before running this, I

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:34.290
might actually compile it or
convert it to this much more

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:35.670
cryptic-looking format.

00:18:35.670 --> 00:18:38.550
This is not code that I, myself,
would particularly enjoy writing.

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:42.330
But this is what's outputted
when you write Python code.

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:44.790
And it's outputted as
something called bytecode.

00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:47.810
So this is just an intermediate format
that still is somewhat readable.

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:50.380
It's at least alphabetical
characters and not zeros and ones.

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:52.860
So as a human I kind
of sort of can read it.

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:55.180
But my computer can
absolutely read this.

00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:58.830
Specifically, a program called
an Interpreter on my computer,

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:01.860
that, I could download to my
Mac or PC or some other device

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:04.660
can understand these
lines one at a time.

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:06.810
And so generally, a
language like Python might

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:10.800
again, be compiled to an intermediate
language like this here, this bytecode.

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:14.760
And then it's this code, ultimately,
that's read by the machine.

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:17.670
But the takeaway for us
is really, quite simply,

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:20.340
that there are different
ways of doing the same thing

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:21.750
in any number of languages.

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:25.210
And even among those languages, do
they get used in different ways?

00:19:25.210 --> 00:19:29.040
Sometimes you have to compile your
code and then run the resulting machine

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:31.410
code, the zeros and ones,
by double clicking an icon

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:32.940
or typing some command.

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:36.420
Or might you be able to write your
code and not explicitly compile

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:38.190
it, just instead interpret it.

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:40.320
The steps might feel or
be a little different.

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:44.850
But at the end of the day, most
users experience the software

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:47.400
in a familiar way, by
double-clicking an icon

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:49.290
by running in the name of a command.

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:52.110
And for all intents and purposes,
your users or your customers

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:55.540
don't need to know or care what
language something is written in,
